Standards review complete.

## Standards

All findings are judgement calls — the repo documents no standards, so this rests on the Fowler ch.3 baseline alone. Overall the code is small, well-named, and the comments carry real intent. Four findings, none severe.

**1. Primitive Obsession — `src/scenario.ts`, item types as bare `string`** (moderate)

```ts
export interface Item { type: string; ... }
export interface Damage { itemType: string; amount: number; }
const BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = { sword: 100, ... };
```
The set of insurable kinds is closed and known at compile time, yet it lives as `string` with a runtime `assertKnownItems` guard (`if (!(item.type in BASE_PREMIUMS))`). A `type ItemType = "sword" | "amulet" | ...` would make `BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type]` total instead of implicitly `number | undefined`, and confine parsing to the CLI boundary. Also note `Item.material` is declared and never read by the engine — the "dragon material full reimbursement" rule turns out to be the default path, so the field is data the domain does not use.

**2. Duplicated Code — two parallel item-type tables** (moderate)

```ts
const BASE_PREMIUMS = { sword: 100, amulet: 60, staff: 80, potion: 40, rune: 25, moonstone: 25 };
const INSURANCE_VALUES = { sword: 1000, amulet: 600, staff: 800, potion: 400, rune: 250, moonstone: 250 };
```
Same key set, same shape, exactly ×10 throughout, plus `COMPONENT_TYPES` as a third partial table over the same domain. Adding an item type is a three-place edit — a whiff of Shotgun Surgery in miniature. One `ITEMS: Record<ItemType, { premium: number; insuredValue: number; isComponent: boolean }>` would collapse all three and make the ×10 relation explicit or deliberate.

**3. Primitive Obsession / Mysterious Name — policy handles are step indices** (minor)

```ts
policies.set(index, openPolicy(step.items));
const policy = policies.get(step.policy)!;
```
`ClaimStep.policy: number` is a step index doing duty as a policy id, and the `!` asserts a lookup the type system doesn't guarantee — an out-of-range `policy` is an unchecked crash, while sibling invalid inputs (unknown type, uncovered damage, negative amount) all raise `ScenarioError`. Inconsistent rejection path more than a naming issue.

**4. Feature Envy — `reimbursement` and `settleClaim` reach into `Item`/`Policy`** (minor, arguably fine)

```ts
function settleClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {
  ...
  policy.remainingCap -= payout;
```
Free functions mutating a passed-in record's field. In a deliberately data-oriented module this is a defensible style choice, not a defect; flagging only because the baseline names it.

**Not found:** Repeated Switches (the single `step.op === "claim"` branch occurs once), Speculative Generality (no unused hooks or params — `material` is the closest, see 1), Message Chains, Middle Man, Refused Bequest, Divergent Change. Test files read cleanly; `premiumFor` and `claimOn` are good extractions of the repeated arrange shape.

